Chapter: ProbeShowTypes
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 `show` is polymorphic. The plug implements one of its four cases.

 Its declared type is `ForAllTy 0 (FunTy (TypeVar 0) empty-row TextTy)` --
 anything to Text -- and bare metal honours that by dispatching on the
 argument's resolved type (`emit-show-builtin`, Emit/X86_64.codex:1639):

     Real f32   -> emit-show-real-approx
     Real       -> __real_to_text
     Text       -> the value ITSELF, unchanged
     Boolean    -> "True" or "False"     (emit-show-bool, :1663)
     otherwise  -> __itoa

 The plug emits `cx_show_int(<arg>)` for all of them, and `cx_show_int` takes
 an `i64`. So `show` of a Text or a Boolean is not a wrong answer, it is a zig
 type error -- which is at least loud, but loud in the wrong register.

 THAT IS THE POINT OF THIS PROBE rather than the arithmetic. The failure is
 `error: expected type 'i64', found '[]const u8'`, pointing into a generated
 line thousands of columns wide, naming no Codex construct. It is NOT a
 `@compileError("zig plug: ...")` marker, and two mechanisms read only markers:
 `zig-is-unmapped` decides recoverability by testing for one, and
 `corpus_run.py --transpile` histograms them to rank WHICH GAP TO FIX FIRST. A
 gap that fails this way scores zero in the ranking that sets priorities, no
 matter how often it bites. Same class as the type-variable leak in
 probe-tyvar-leak.codex, reached from a different direction.

 `show` has 108 call sites in the compiler. They evidently pass integers,
 since the ladder builds -- so the question this probe raises is not "is the
 plug broken today" but "what happens the first time somebody writes
 `show` of a Boolean", and the answer should be a named refusal.

 Bare metal prints all five lines below. The zig arm is expected not to
 compile at all, which is why this cannot live in a tier file.

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni "-- show of an integer: the case the plug implements --"
   print-line-uni ("show 42                    : " & show 42)
   print-line-uni ("show -7                    : " & show (0 - 7))
   print-line-uni ("agrees with integer-to-text: " & (if text-compare (show 42) (integer-to-text 42) == 0 then "yes" else "no"))
   print-line-uni "-- show of a Boolean: bare metal says True or False --"
   print-line-uni ("show True                  : " & show True)
   print-line-uni ("show False                 : " & show False)
   print-line-uni "-- show of a Text: bare metal returns it unchanged --"
   print-line-uni ("show of a text             : " & show "already text")
   print-line-uni "-- reached the end, so this arm dispatched on the type --"
  end
